---
// src/pages/blue-staffy-blog-guides/index.astro — REBUILT from the approved board record
// (data/boards/blue-staffy-blog-guides.json, approved 2026-09-20).
// Hand-written and NOT regenerated by `npm run extract`: the slug is listed in
// data/facts/rebuilt.json, so `migration_parity.py` skips it and `facts_preserved_check.py`,
// `link_parity_check.py` and `verbatim_set_check.py` are its gates instead.
//
// THIS URL USED TO SERVE A BLOG POST'S BODY, AND THAT IS THE THING THIS BUILD FIXES. The
// migrated archive was an index page with a post's front matter: `src/content/blog/
// blue-staffy-blog-guides.md` claimed THIS slug, so `src/pages/[...post].astro` built the
// route and a reader who came for the guides index was served one post's two archive entries
// and nothing else (data/page-map.json: word_count 0, defects empty-h1 and stub). The post is
// now `src/content/blog/how-to-choose-the-right-blue-staffy-puppy-for-your-family.md`, at a
// slug of its own, and this file is the hub. NO REDIRECT IS OWED: the post never had a URL of
// its own to move — its `canonical` was this one — and this URL still answers 200, with the
// same title intent and the whole of its verbatim set on it. A 301 here would be a redirect
// away from a page that did not go anywhere.
//
// THE STRATEGY IS "THE READING LIST" (`brief.strategy`). Of the three hubs scanned, two are
// bare card grids with no dates and no authors; this one says what the set is, who wrote it,
// and how big it actually is — one post and four pages worth starting with — rather than
// dressing four links as an archive.
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE MAY NOT SAY, and none of it is an oversight (`brief.out_of_scope`): no
// publication cadence of any kind, because no file on disk holds a schedule and a blog index
// inventing one is the easiest false promise available to it; no byline but Lisa Bright; no
// tags, categories, month archive or search box over a collection of one; no newsletter
// block; no enquiry form (the contact page carries the one form the form audit measures, and
// `talk-to-us` links it); no puppy grid and no prices; and NO LINK TO /blog/, the site's other
// index of the same collection — `dropped.links` strikes it by name, because two indexes of
// one collection linking each other is a loop.
//
// WORKING RULE 11. The one photograph is a puppy master already served by the site, at its
// original public path with its original alt text; the migrated archive carried no image of
// its own (data/page-map.json records images 0), so the hero borrows rather than commissions.
// NO srcset: nothing is baked beside that master, and `bakedSrcset` refuses to name a
// candidate that is not on disk.
//
// WORKING RULE 14. `data/page-map.json` records embeds 0 for this URL and data/facts/
// blue-staffy-blog-guides.json's `embeds` list is empty, so there is no id to carry and none
// is minted. No `VideoEmbed`, and no VideoObject.
//
// WORKING RULE 15 (faithful rewrite). The VERBATIM SET is four elements and they are READ OFF
// THE RECORD rather than retyped here: the two archive entry headings, each repaired in
// `verbatim.changed` because it collided with the live H1 of the very page it linked, and the
// "/ Puppy Buying Guide UK /" category line that opened both. All four are in `start-here`.
// THE H1 IS NOT A DEVIATION AND OWES NO ROW: data/verbatim/blue-staffy-blog-guides.json's
// `h1` is the EMPTY STRING — the migrated archive had no H1 at all (defect `empty-h1`) — so
// there is no wording to carry, the set's element list skips it, and the board schema will
// not accept a `verbatim.changed` row with an empty `old` anyway. `h1.pick` is variant 0 and
// renders as picked. The slug's own phrase is not inside it, so it is stated once, in the
// eyebrow.
//
// WORKING RULE 16. The hero is `H-BL3` and the counter is `C-BL2`, the blog family's own
// arrangements, resolved through `pickedStyle()`. THE HERO DEGRADES, DELIBERATELY AND
// VISIBLY: H-BL3 is a photo MOSAIC on a steel band, and `Hero` renders a mosaic only from two
// or more tiles ("the arrangement degrades, it never invents a tile"). This record bakes ONE
// asset, so the band carries that one photograph above the copy. Inventing a second tile out
// of a puppy photo this page has no record for is the thing rule 11 and rule 9 both forbid,
// and a one-tile mosaic is a photo with extra markup. The counter is the ruled-column strip
// on the band, which no other page takes.
//
// TWO FIGURES, BOTH DERIVED AT BUILD AND BOTH ASSERTED AGAINST THE RECORD (amendment 10.5).
// The post count is the number of markdown files in the `blog` collection, counted by
// `import.meta.glob` rather than typed; the start-here count is the length of that section's
// own tree. A reading that no longer matches what the breeder approved throws with both
// numbers in the message.
//
// H4-H6 ARE WRITTEN HERE, not in the record (amendment 2.1): five ladders, five H5 and five
// H6, every H6 on the record's own `tuple.h6_prefixes` entry, "Guide Note:". Every heading
// renders through `titleCase()` (amendment 4b).
//
// THE SECTION `top` RENDERS NO H2. Its record heading IS `h1.variants[0]` word for word, and
// a page printing the same sentence as an H1 and again as an H2 under it has said it twice;
// /uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/, whose record has the same pair, resolves it the same way.
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/kit/Hero.astro';
import CounterStrip from '../../components/kit/CounterStrip.astro';
import InfoCard from '../../components/kit/InfoCard.astro';
import Testimonial from '../../components/kit/Testimonial.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/kit/Faq.astro';
import PageDate from '../../components/kit/PageDate.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { sectionsFromRecord } from '../../lib/sections';
import { pickedStyle } from '../../lib/pickedStyle';
import { bakedAssets } from '../../lib/assets';
import { recordText, countedFigures, sourceKey, ledgeText,
  sentenceAnchor as S, type LedgeEntry, type StatRow } from '../../lib/recordText';
import { boxClass } from '../../lib/boardStyles';
import { titleCase as T } from '../../lib/headings';
import { loadFaq } from '../../lib/faq';
import { SITE, abs } from '../../lib/site';
import reviewsJson from '../../../data/reviews.json';
import record from '../../../data/boards/blue-staffy-blog-guides.json';

const path = '/blue-staffy-blog-guides/';
// The picked meta variants and H1, BY INDEX from the approval — never retyped here.
const title = record.meta_set.titles[record.approval!.meta!.title];
const description = record.meta_set.descriptions[record.approval!.meta!.description];
const h1 = record.h1.variants[record.approval!.h1];

const { sec, heading, node, nodeOpening } = recordText(record);
const asset = bakedAssets(record);
const heroPhoto = asset('guides-hub-hero');

// ── THE POSTS, FROM THE COLLECTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The cards are rows of the `blog` content collection, newest first, exactly as
// src/pages/blog/index.astro reads them. Their hrefs are DATA, not anchors the breeder wrote
// on the board, which is why `sections[latest-guides].links` is empty and why
// `link_parity_check.py` exempts them by name — the same argument, and the same shape of
// exemption, as the puppy grid's cards (spec §9 amendment 6b).
const posts = (await getCollection('blog'))
  .sort((a, b) => (b.data.date ?? '').localeCompare(a.data.date ?? ''));

// ── THE TWO FIGURES, COUNTED RATHER THAN COPIED ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// `#files(*.md)` is counted off the directory itself rather than off `posts`: the record's
// source names the FILES, and a glob is the reading that stays true if a post is ever drafted
// without being published. The two happen to agree today, and the assertion below is what
// says so out loud.
const MD_FILES = Object.keys(import.meta.glob('../../content/blog/*.md', { eager: false }));
const startHereTree = sec('start-here').tree ?? [];
const COUNTED: Record<string, number> = {
  'src/content/blog#files(*.md)': MD_FILES.length,
  'data/boards/blue-staffy-blog-guides.json#len(sections[start-here].tree)': startHereTree.length,
};
const counted = countedFigures(COUNTED, 'blue-staffy-blog-guides');
const statRows = (sec('stats') as unknown as { stats: StatRow[] }).stats;
for (const row of statRows) {
  if (!(sourceKey(row.source) in COUNTED)) {
    throw new Error(`blue-staffy-blog-guides: the counter row "${row.label}" cites `
      + `${row.source}, which this page derives nothing from`);
  }
}
const counterStats = statRows.map(counted);
// The card list and the counter must be the same reading of the same collection: a hub that
// prints "1 post" over two cards is the exact defect the derivation was supposed to prevent.
if (posts.length !== MD_FILES.length) {
  throw new Error(`blue-staffy-blog-guides: the blog collection resolves ${posts.length} `
    + `entr(y/ies) and src/content/blog holds ${MD_FILES.length} markdown file(s) — the card `
    + 'list and the counter would print two different readings of one collection');
}

// ── THE HERO'S OWN DATA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// H-BL3's ledge is `chips`, so the record's two approved chips are what sits under the lede.
// The record ALSO carries a `hero.aside` — a three-line "Start here" contents list — and this
// arrangement has no aside slot to put it in. It is not dropped: its three lines are the first
// three destinations of `start-here` below, which is the section that owns them and links them.
const heroSection = sec('top') as unknown as
  { hero: { chips: LedgeEntry[]; aside: { title: string; items: LedgeEntry[] } } };
const heroChips = heroSection.hero.chips.map(ledgeText);

// ── THE REVIEW, BY NAME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The record's intent states MODE: single on data/reviews.json entry 2 — Mark J, London —
// because it is the only one of the three that describes reading before buying, which is what
// a guides hub is for. WHICH review is the record's sentence inside `record_hash`; HOW the
// block is drawn is the pick, and `approval.picks` picks S2 (`mode: 'grid'`) over the note's
// argument for S1. THE PICK BEATS THE NOTE — the lesson 645a988 and 8c71fcd both wrote down.
// The note's worry does not apply here: it is about a grid whose other slots render
// REVIEW_PLACEHOLDER, and `Testimonial` opens a slot only for a review it was handed, so one
// review passed to a grid is one card and no placeholder.
interface Review { quote: string; name: string; place: string; source: string }
const markJ = (reviewsJson as Review[]).filter((r) => r.name === 'Mark J');
if (markJ.length !== 1) {
  throw new Error('blue-staffy-blog-guides: data/reviews.json no longer holds the Mark J row '
    + 'the record names');
}

// ── THE THREE FAQ ROWS THE RECORD NAMES, BY ID ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Written into data/faq.json in the same commit as this page, which is what the record's own
// note says has to happen: tests/py/test_faq_data.py requires a page-sourced row's `source`
// file to exist and its fact phrases to be on that page, so the rows could not exist before
// the page did. None of the three states a cadence.
const faqSection = sec('questions') as unknown as { tree: { heading: string }[] };
const faqRows = loadFaq();
const faqs = faqSection.tree.map((n) => {
  const row = faqRows.find((r) => r.id === n.heading);
  if (!row) throw new Error(`blue-staffy-blog-guides: data/faq.json has no row ${n.heading}`);
  return row;
});

const sections = sectionsFromRecord(record);
const style = (id: string) => pickedStyle(record, id);
const box = (id: string) => boxClass(style(id));
const heroStyle = style('top');
const counterStyle = style('stats');
const reviewStyle = style('owner-review');

const town = SITE.location_label.split(' · ')[0];
const hours = SITE.hours.replace('Mo-Su ', 'from ').replace('-', ' to ');

// CollectionPage + FAQPage (`brief.schema.types`). BaseLayout already emits the `#webpage`
// node with the git-derived dates, so the CollectionPage is the one minted here and it carries
// no date of its own: BaseLayout drops its WebPage node the moment a page's schema mentions
// one, and a reference to a node nobody emitted is the dangling `@id` `schema_check.py` blocks
// on. `mainEntity` is the post list — the same rows the cards render, so the markup and the
// page cannot describe two different collections. No BlogPosting here: the post's own page
// mints that, which is the other half of moving it off this URL.
const WEBPAGE = { '@id': `${path}#webpage` };
const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#collection`,
    name: h1,
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en-GB',
    isPartOf: WEBPAGE,
    mainEntityOfPage: WEBPAGE,
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#business` },
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'ItemList',
      numberOfItems: posts.length,
      itemListElement: posts.map((p, i) => ({
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: i + 1,
        url: abs(`/${p.data.slug}/`),
        name: p.data.title.split(' | ')[0],
      })),
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: r.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: r.a },
    })),
  },
];
---
<PageShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={path}
  ogType="website"
  schema={pageSchema}
  sections={sections}
>
  {/* 01 top — hero, picked style H-BL3 (the mosaic band; one baked tile, so the band carries
      the single photograph above centred copy, with the record's two chips under the lede).
      No CTA buttons and no trust chips beyond the two the record approved (amendment 6a). The
      recorded refresh delta is the LAYOUT: a post-card band with the counts beneath it, which
      no guide page hero does. NO H2 — see the file header. */}
  <section slot="hero" id="top" data-section-label="The guides index" class={box('top')}>
    <Hero
      as="h1"
      eyebrow={`Blue Staffy blog guides · ${SITE.location_label}`}
      title={T(h1)}
      {/* ONE SENTENCE. The desktop lede is `-webkit-line-clamp: 2` and a clamp HIDES its
          overflow, so a third line disappears rather than pushing anything past the ceiling. */}
      lede="Everything we have written about living with a blue Staffy."
      image={heroPhoto.file}
      imageAlt={heroPhoto.alt}
      imageWidth={heroPhoto.w}
      imageHeight={heroPhoto.h}
      chips={heroChips}
      layout={heroStyle.layout.hero ?? 'mosaic'}
      align={heroStyle.layout.align ?? 'center'}
      media={heroStyle.layout.media ?? 'top'}
      ledge={heroStyle.layout.ledge ?? 'chips'}
    />
    <p>
      I am {SITE.breeder_name} and I raise blue Staffies in {town}. This is an index of the
      writing, not a sales page: the long guides answer what the breed is, what we screen for
      and what to ask a breeder. Every figure in them is read off a file we keep.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* 02 takeaways — takeaways, picked style S3 (the rail of statement cards beside the
      heading). FOUR cards, which is what the record's tree carries, each a fact about this set
      of pages rather than a claim about puppies. The recorded refresh delta is the layout. */}
  <section id="takeaways" data-section-label="What you will find here" class={box('takeaways')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('takeaways'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">Four things worth knowing before you spend a click.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <div class="bl-cards">
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 0).heading)}
          body="The breed guide, the health page and the buying guide are where the substance is."
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 1).heading)}
          body={`The collection holds ${counterStats[0].n} entry today, and the list below shows it unpadded.`}
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 2).heading)}
          body={`${SITE.breeder_name} writes them, about her own dogs, and says so at the top of each one.`}
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 3).heading)}
          body="Prices, the deposit and the delivery band are read out of data, never typed into prose."
        />
      </div>

      <h4>{T('A Small Set, Said Plainly')}</h4>
      <p>
        Four pages and one post is a short reading list, and a hub that dressed it up as an
        archive would be the first thing a reader caught.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('Length Is Not The Point')}</h5>
      <p>
        The guides are long where the subject is and stop where it stops. None of them pads to
        a word count.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Guide Note: Read the health page before the buying guide')}</h6>
      <p>It is the one that tells you what the certificates you are about to ask for actually say.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 03 stats — the counter strip, picked style C-BL2 (ruled columns on a steel band, the
      label under each figure): the blog family's arrangement, and no other page takes it. Both
      figures are derived at build and checked against the number the breeder approved. The
      recorded refresh delta is the density — two figures, given a whole band. */}
  <section id="stats" data-section-label="What is here so far" class={box('stats')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('stats'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <CounterStrip
        stats={counterStats}
        tiles={counterStyle.layout.tiles ?? 'ruled'}
        label={counterStyle.layout.label ?? 'under'}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 04 latest-guides — standard, picked style S1 (prose with the entries as cards on the
      surface). ONE CARD PER ENTRY in the `blog` collection, generated at build: the card hrefs
      are collection data and carry no link rows of their own, which is what the record's note
      argues and what the link-parity exemption records. The recorded refresh delta is the
      container. */}
  <section id="latest-guides" data-section-label="The latest guide" class={box('latest-guides')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('latest-guides'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">Every post we have published, newest first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <div class="bl-cards">
        {posts.map((p) => (
          <article class="kit-card post-card">
            <h3><a href={`/${p.data.slug}/`}>{p.data.title.split(' | ')[0]}</a></h3>
            <p>{p.data.description}</p>
            <PageDate path={`/${p.data.slug}/`} />
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h3>{T(node('latest-guides', 0).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        One post, and the list above is all of it. We will not say when the next lands:
        nothing we keep records a schedule.
      </p>

      <h3>{T(node('latest-guides', 1).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        The post carries no published date, so the card shows the last updated date from our
        committed history.
      </p>

      <h4>{T('A Card Says What A Heading Cannot')}</h4>
      <p>
        The old version of this URL listed its entries as two linked headings with no dates and
        no descriptions, which is a heading list pretending to be an index.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('One Entry Is Still An Index')}</h5>
      <p>
        A list of one is honest. A grid of one repeated four times to fill a row is not, and
        that is the only reason this section looks short.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Guide Note: The date is the repository’s, not a marketing stamp')}</h6>
      <p>It moves when the file moves, and never because somebody wanted the page to look fresh.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 05 start-here — standard, picked style S2 (image-above two-column prose). FOUR link-first
      sentences (rules/links.md), one per destination. THE WHOLE VERBATIM SET IS HERE: nodes 2
      and 3 are the migrated archive's own two entry headings, each repaired in
      `verbatim.changed` because it collided with the live H1 of the page it linked, and each
      carries the archive's own category line "/ Puppy Buying Guide UK /" as its opening. Both
      are read off the record rather than retyped. The recorded refresh delta is the motif. */}
  <section id="start-here" data-section-label="Start here" class={box('start-here')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('start-here'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        Four pages, in the order they are worth reading. The old archive promised a roadmap
        through choosing ethical breeders, health screening, delivery and temperament, then
        linked two pages; these are where those subjects are written.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <h3>{T(node('start-here', 0).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide/">{S('read the Staffordshire Bull Terrier breed guide')}</a> for
        what the breed is, what a day with one asks for, and where it stands in UK law. It is
        the page to read if you are still deciding whether this is your dog at all.
      </p>

      <h3>{T(node('start-here', 1).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="/blue-staffy-health-uk/">{S("read what we test for and what a Staffy's health looks like")}</a> —
        the two DNA tests our parent dogs are recorded clear of, the vaccination course, and
        what the breed's life expectancy is.
      </p>

      <h3>{T(node('start-here', 2).heading)}</h3>
      <p>{nodeOpening('start-here', 2)}</p>
      <p>
        <a href="/uk-blue-staffy-puppy-buying-guide/">{S('read the buying guide before you contact any breeder')}</a>:
        the steps, the questions to put to whoever is selling, the red flags and the first
        thirty days at home. That includes putting the questions to us.
      </p>

      <h3>{T(node('start-here', 3).heading)}</h3>
      <p>{nodeOpening('start-here', 3)}</p>
      <p>
        <a href="/buy-staffy-puppies-for-sale-uk/">{S('what a purchase from us comes with, in writing')}</a> sets
        out the contract, the paperwork and the delivery arrangement.
      </p>

      <h4>{T('The Order Is Deliberate')}</h4>
      <p>
        Breed first, then health, then the buying process: each one assumes you have read the
        one before it, and none of them repeats the others.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('Two Of These Headings Are The Old Page’s Own')}</h5>
      <p>
        They were the archive's two entries, and they are carried word for word bar one noun
        apiece — each collided with the live heading of the page it pointed at, and the
        record's own accounting says which word moved and why.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Guide Note: None of the four is a summary of another')}</h6>
      <p>Where two pages touch a subject, one owns it and the other links it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 06 owner-review — reviews, picked style S2 as the bed and as the mode (the grid on a
      steel band), read off the StyleDef rather than hard-coded. ONE review, Mark J of London,
      named in the record's own intent. The recorded refresh delta is the accent. */}
  <section id="owner-review" data-section-label="One reader, after the research" class={box('owner-review')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">The case for reading first</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('owner-review'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <Testimonial mode={reviewStyle.layout.mode ?? 'grid'} reviews={markJ} />
      <p>
        This page asks for an hour of reading before anybody writes to us. One family, named,
        saying they did exactly that is the only evidence on disk that it is worth the hour.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 07 questions — faq, picked style S1 (the plain accordion). THREE rows of data/faq.json in
      the record's order, added in this commit with a fact phrase each. Not one of them names a
      cadence: `blog-updates` answers with the last-updated date the pages actually render
      instead. The accordion's answers sit outside this section's word band (amendment 4a). */}
  <section id="questions" data-section-label="Questions about these guides" class={box('questions')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('questions'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">
        Three questions people put to a page like this one, each of them answered from
        something with a heading on it further up.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <p>
        One asks how often we add a guide, and the answer says plainly that we publish no
        schedule. Nothing we keep holds one.
      </p>
      <Faq items={faqs} />

      <h4>{T('The Missing Row Is Missing Deliberately')}</h4>
      <p>
        There is no row about how many guides are coming, for the reason the answer above
        gives.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('Nothing Down Here Is New')}</h5>
      <p>
        Each answer restates something with a heading on it further up. A claim that lives only
        behind a disclosure triangle is a claim somebody chose not to say out loud.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Guide Note: Three rows is the whole list')}</h6>
      <p>Anything a fourth would have covered already has a heading of its own further up.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 08 talk-to-us — standard, NO PICK (`options.pick` is null), and the close. The email
      address and the hours are read out of data/settings.json rather than typed. NO FORM: the
      contact page carries the one form the form audit measures, and this section links it. The
      recorded refresh delta is the layout. */}
